    {
      "id": "MSC-03",
      "chapter": "normalization",
      "anchor": "normalization-raising-casimir-gap",
      "title": "The opposite-sign gap bounds the mixed term on the raising part",
      "statement": "For Λ=λ+ρ_n−ρ_c, the mixed eigenvalue for a<b of opposite signs is (λ_a+ρ_n,a)−(λ_b+ρ_n,b)+1−m/2. If λ_a−λ_b≥γ for all such pairs, the real quadratic form of Ω on the raising part is bounded below by (γ−2m) times squared norm. Symmetric derivatives of positive order j then have mixed Casimir contribution at least 2j(γ−2m) times squared norm when their slices lie in the raising part.",
      "hypotheses": "λ is in decreasing parameter order. Strict positivity in the jet application requires γ>2m, while the arithmetic lower bound itself uses only the weak pairwise gap. Each symmetric one-position slice must belong to the raising subspace.",
      "correspondence": "Expanding the difference of two Casimir scalars produces the stated eigenvalue. Each coordinate of ρ_n is between −m/2 and m/2. Orthogonality of distinct highest weights extends the lower bound to their sum. The symmetric-array result uses the sum of the squared slice norms and the two identical cross terms, followed by the separate nonnegative position Casimir.",
      "dependencies": [
        "MSC-01",
        "MSC-02"
      ],
      "equations": [
        "normalization-raising-shift"
      ],
